WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:10.163
Well, there it is.

00:00:11.130 --> 00:00:15.113
My beloved California
absolutely blanketed by smoke.

00:00:18.530 --> 00:00:22.603
Somehow both apocalyptic and
beautiful at the same time.

00:00:25.690 --> 00:00:27.380
Some would say it's not the best idea

00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:28.833
to head right into this,

00:00:30.970 --> 00:00:33.283
least of all during a freaking pandemic.

00:00:41.450 --> 00:00:44.460
But here I am, with my mask collection,

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:47.020
double pumps of hand sanitizer,

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:51.950
in my RV bubble that contains
these two fine companions.

00:00:51.950 --> 00:00:54.300
Like me, they've been tested,

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:56.090
layered with protocols

00:00:56.090 --> 00:00:58.443
and chosen to interact with the world.

00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:04.233
Our mission is to head out on Route 395.

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:08.560
It's a road that runs down
California's eastern spine,

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:11.993
and that offers up unfair
amounts of spectacular sites,

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:16.190
the kinds of things that
still produce wonder

00:01:16.190 --> 00:01:17.693
amid a smoky haze.

00:01:18.550 --> 00:01:19.993
I mean, come on!

00:01:23.410 --> 00:01:24.980
Along the way we'll be visiting some

00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:27.330
of the tech world's outliers,

00:01:27.330 --> 00:01:30.273
people that like to live
and work on the frontier.

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:35.623
They're folks trying to
feed the planet, fix it,

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:39.230
or if need be,

00:01:39.230 --> 00:01:40.253
just get off it.

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:46.760
If thinking different is your thing,

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:48.840
then here we go.

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:50.520
Show us the way, rented RV,

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:53.320
with your included dashboard ornament.

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:54.643
Show us the way.

00:02:11.835 --> 00:02:13.918
We're going 45 in a 70.

00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:16.161
Is that as fast as it goes?

00:02:16.161 --> 00:02:19.270
No, it's just going uphill, I'd say.

00:02:19.270 --> 00:02:22.100
If you're gonna drive an RV
through a fiery hellscape

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:23.830
during a pandemic,

00:02:23.830 --> 00:02:26.210
you wanna be sure and
do it right alongside

00:02:26.210 --> 00:02:28.750
a massive active fault system.

00:02:28.750 --> 00:02:32.140
In this case, may I
present the Walker Lane,

00:02:32.140 --> 00:02:35.403
which is often found hugging Route 395.

00:02:37.090 --> 00:02:40.100
While not as well known as,
say, the San Andreas fault,

00:02:40.100 --> 00:02:44.060
it's the Walker Lane that
gives 395 much of it's charm.

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:45.700
We're talking hot springs,

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:48.103
extinct volcanoes and mountains.

00:02:49.450 --> 00:02:51.890
And we're talking more apocalypse,

00:02:51.890 --> 00:02:54.000
because it's now thought
that the Walker Lane

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:57.413
may result in Reno ending
up as a beach town.

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:02.960
Who predicts such things?

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:05.550
Well, for one, this man.

00:03:05.550 --> 00:03:07.770
Geologist James Faulds,

00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:09.980
who forced me to climb this hill near Reno

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:12.060
to learn about the fault.

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:15.310
We are in the middle of
what's called the Walker Lane,

00:03:15.310 --> 00:03:18.530
and it's a big belt of
faults in Western Nevada,

00:03:18.530 --> 00:03:20.060
also Eastern California.

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:24.040
The San Andreas accommodates
about 80% of the motion

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:26.530
between the Pacific and
North American plates,

00:03:26.530 --> 00:03:30.190
and about 20, maybe as
much as 25% of that motion

00:03:30.190 --> 00:03:32.760
is over here on the Walker Lane.

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:35.210
Faulds is one of a growing
number of geologists

00:03:35.210 --> 00:03:38.460
who've become obsessed
by the Walker Lane fault

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:39.300
and what it could mean

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:41.590
for residents of California and Nevada

00:03:41.590 --> 00:03:43.720
in the distant future.

00:03:43.720 --> 00:03:45.980
People focus so much on
the San Andreas fault

00:03:45.980 --> 00:03:48.700
and what this could
mean in 10 million years

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:52.470
to the geography of the
United States and California,

00:03:52.470 --> 00:03:53.860
but there is the potential

00:03:53.860 --> 00:03:57.800
that this fault could
be even more dramatic

00:03:57.800 --> 00:03:59.230
if some of these theories are right.

00:03:59.230 --> 00:04:01.890
We know the Walker Lane
is growing northward,

00:04:01.890 --> 00:04:03.590
along with the San Andreas,

00:04:03.590 --> 00:04:06.210
so they are scheduled
to meet somewhere off

00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:07.360
the Southern Oregon coast

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:09.724
in about maybe seven
or eight million years.

00:04:09.724 --> 00:04:11.343
And what does that look like?

00:04:12.211 --> 00:04:13.700
Well, when that happens,

00:04:13.700 --> 00:04:16.610
what some of us think will occur

00:04:16.610 --> 00:04:19.540
is that the entire San Andreas
then will rip up through

00:04:19.540 --> 00:04:23.710
the western part of
North America, or the US,

00:04:23.710 --> 00:04:27.320
and it'll stray on
California off to the west.

00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:30.100
I mean, the Pacific Ocean ends
up kind of rushing through

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:31.700
parts of Nevada, right?

00:04:31.700 --> 00:04:34.523
Well,
Or is this controversial?

00:04:35.963 --> 00:04:36.796
That will be a slow process,

00:04:36.796 --> 00:04:39.240
but we have beach-front
property in Reno someday.

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:42.560
This fault also brings along
some opportunities, right?

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:44.710
We have these crustal
motions in the region,

00:04:44.710 --> 00:04:47.420
and in Nevada there's a
stretching of the crust,

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:49.510
stretching in the horizontal dimension.

00:04:49.510 --> 00:04:52.550
And that helps to kinda
dilate or open up faults,

00:04:52.550 --> 00:04:54.930
and that brings hot water
up closer to the surface,

00:04:54.930 --> 00:04:57.553
and then that can be tapped
for geothermal energy.

00:04:59.730 --> 00:05:00.880
What's that, Jim?

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:03.460
Did you say geothermal energy?

00:05:03.460 --> 00:05:06.740
Well, then, let's have a
brief musical interlude

00:05:06.740 --> 00:05:10.590
as we gaze upon the nearby
geothermal plant of Ormat.

00:05:10.590 --> 00:05:11.900
It's got everything.

00:05:11.900 --> 00:05:15.350
Awesome fans, I mean really awesome fans,

00:05:15.350 --> 00:05:17.580
wells that tap into geothermal brine

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:19.910
10,000 feet underground,

00:05:19.910 --> 00:05:23.330
sweet, sweet pipes and transmission lines,

00:05:23.330 --> 00:05:25.100
turbines that you can't really see

00:05:25.100 --> 00:05:26.963
on either side of this generator,

00:05:28.290 --> 00:05:31.910
enough juice for 22,000 homes.

00:05:31.910 --> 00:05:34.740
The Walker Lane fault in action.

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:35.823
Back to you, Jim.

00:05:37.060 --> 00:05:40.300
And then part of the reason
there was any controversy,

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:43.194
or we didn't even know
that much about this fault,

00:05:43.194 --> 00:05:46.190
was 20 years ago it was
much harder to gather data

00:05:46.190 --> 00:05:47.090
about this kinda thing.

00:05:47.090 --> 00:05:50.040
The fault wasn't as clear
when you looked down.

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:52.070
And so what is this device?

00:05:52.070 --> 00:05:53.850
This is a GPS station.

00:05:53.850 --> 00:05:56.440
So it's actually
communicating with satellites.

00:05:56.440 --> 00:05:58.657
Its position is changing through time,

00:05:58.657 --> 00:06:01.240
and those satellites are
tracking those changes

00:06:01.240 --> 00:06:03.200
down to the scale of millimeters.

00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:04.490
How many are monitoring the fault?

00:06:04.490 --> 00:06:07.030
There's 400 of these
sensors in this region

00:06:07.030 --> 00:06:08.890
in a network called Magnet.

00:06:08.890 --> 00:06:12.680
These stations allow us
to focus in on maybe what

00:06:12.680 --> 00:06:16.140
the most important part
of the region might be.

00:06:16.140 --> 00:06:21.010
Like, how much activity are
people around here seeing

00:06:21.010 --> 00:06:22.620
as result of the fault?

00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:25.680
Nevada is the third most
seismically active state,

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:27.433
after Alaska and California.

00:06:30.990 --> 00:06:33.110
After our chat James drove off

00:06:33.110 --> 00:06:36.200
and left me with that unique
brand of existential dread

00:06:36.200 --> 00:06:37.450
that comes from learning about

00:06:37.450 --> 00:06:39.560
the perils of your surroundings

00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:41.803
and what will befall humankind.

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:46.920
To get rid of all that
I had only one option,

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:47.900
a night spent

00:06:47.900 --> 00:06:52.890
at the splendid Grand Sierra
Resort and Casino RV Park,

00:06:52.890 --> 00:06:54.570
where, as it turns out,

00:06:54.570 --> 00:06:58.113
you can still get DoorDash
and eat your sorrows away.

00:07:01.610 --> 00:07:03.160
Right, let's see what it says.

00:07:03.160 --> 00:07:04.310
Define JB.

00:07:04.310 --> 00:07:06.470
Google maps will get you
close to the mailbox,

00:07:06.470 --> 00:07:08.150
but not exactly there.

00:07:08.150 --> 00:07:10.623
Expect a long gravel driveway.

00:07:12.430 --> 00:07:13.263
You got it?

00:07:14.580 --> 00:07:15.413
Yeah.

00:07:16.440 --> 00:07:19.310
From Reno we headed south to Carson City.

00:07:19.310 --> 00:07:22.480
Well, like the outermost
edge of Carson City,

00:07:22.480 --> 00:07:26.820
where an old friend is engineering
a most unique existence.

00:07:26.820 --> 00:07:30.630
Today, we're a little bit
outside of Carson City, Nevada.

00:07:30.630 --> 00:07:34.240
Going to the house of JB Straubel.

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:35.590
He's a guy I've known for a long time.

00:07:35.590 --> 00:07:38.700
He was one of the first
employees at Tesla,

00:07:38.700 --> 00:07:42.160
and the long-time chief
technology officer.

00:07:42.160 --> 00:07:44.670
I always thought of him as
the heart and soul of Tesla,

00:07:44.670 --> 00:07:47.610
because he was this guy who,

00:07:47.610 --> 00:07:49.210
even before Tesla was a real thing,

00:07:49.210 --> 00:07:52.300
he was obsessed with solar powered cars,

00:07:52.300 --> 00:07:55.420
with taking lithium ion batteries

00:07:55.420 --> 00:07:58.610
and using them to one day
reshape the automotive industry.

00:07:58.610 --> 00:08:01.470
He was kind of the dreamer
behind the technology,

00:08:01.470 --> 00:08:03.430
the whole electric car revolution.

00:08:03.430 --> 00:08:04.570
A couple of years ago

00:08:04.570 --> 00:08:07.610
he decided he was gonna go out on his own

00:08:07.610 --> 00:08:10.120
and start a new company
around recycling all

00:08:10.120 --> 00:08:12.770
these lithium ion batteries
that he helped create.

00:08:12.770 --> 00:08:14.910
We're gonna learn a little
bit more about his life,

00:08:14.910 --> 00:08:17.510
and then we're gonna go see
his new factory as well.

00:08:27.490 --> 00:08:29.423
Hey, guys, how are ya?
How are ya?

00:08:30.330 --> 00:08:31.750
I'm good.

00:08:31.750 --> 00:08:33.600
Thanks for letting us do all this.

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:35.330
You've, like, always,

00:08:35.330 --> 00:08:37.660
constantly got some
project going on at home,

00:08:37.660 --> 00:08:39.343
or one or another?

00:08:39.343 --> 00:08:41.540
I mean, I love building things

00:08:41.540 --> 00:08:44.290
and I kinda do this stuff to relax.

00:08:44.290 --> 00:08:45.863
This is the current project.

00:08:46.770 --> 00:08:49.290
Yeah, this is just sort
of a side, fun project,

00:08:49.290 --> 00:08:51.170
but it's an interesting experiment

00:08:51.170 --> 00:08:53.800
in being able to do this
cheaply and quickly,

00:08:53.800 --> 00:08:56.960
and find ways to deploy solar
without heavy equipment.

00:08:56.960 --> 00:08:58.688
And it's just fun.

00:08:58.688 --> 00:09:01.200
And then even though you
ran Tesla's energy division,

00:09:01.200 --> 00:09:06.090
you're building your
own battery pack, right?

00:09:06.090 --> 00:09:06.923
I don't know.

00:09:06.923 --> 00:09:08.880
I find more fun in the process, you know?

00:09:08.880 --> 00:09:10.720
You can pay someone to do something,

00:09:10.720 --> 00:09:12.290
and then it's just done.

00:09:12.290 --> 00:09:14.330
But I think it's almost more the journey

00:09:14.330 --> 00:09:17.900
and the mental exercises
thinking through it.

00:09:17.900 --> 00:09:20.700
JB started coming out here
as Tesla began building

00:09:20.700 --> 00:09:23.193
its massive giga factory battery plant.

00:09:24.530 --> 00:09:27.540
Year by year the charms
of Nevada grew on him

00:09:27.540 --> 00:09:30.120
and he decided to stop living in hotels

00:09:30.120 --> 00:09:31.983
and take over this fine compound.

00:09:33.370 --> 00:09:35.800
Over the years, Elon Musk has gotten all

00:09:35.800 --> 00:09:38.230
of the Tesla love and adoration.

00:09:38.230 --> 00:09:41.930
Meanwhile, JB quietly did
this thing in the background.

00:09:41.930 --> 00:09:44.400
Always a tinkerer and engineer at heart,

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:47.470
he spearheaded many of
Tesla's key projects

00:09:47.470 --> 00:09:50.103
before leaving the company in 2019.

00:09:51.260 --> 00:09:54.970
You come from a long line of tinkerers.

00:09:54.970 --> 00:09:58.950
Tell me a little bit about
your dad and your grandparents.

00:09:58.950 --> 00:10:03.190
My great-grandfather actually
immigrated here from Germany

00:10:03.190 --> 00:10:06.840
and started an engine company
in Green Bay, Wisconsin.

00:10:06.840 --> 00:10:08.940
And he actually built machines,

00:10:08.940 --> 00:10:10.930
some of the very first
internal combustion engines

00:10:10.930 --> 00:10:13.130
for boats back then, for fishing boats.

00:10:13.130 --> 00:10:15.230
I mean, it must be in
the blood to some degree,

00:10:15.230 --> 00:10:17.610
'cause you were tinkering
from, like, what?

00:10:17.610 --> 00:10:19.800
When you were a teenager,
or even before that?

00:10:19.800 --> 00:10:20.880
I just enjoy it.

00:10:20.880 --> 00:10:23.980
It wasn't necessarily a means to an end,

00:10:23.980 --> 00:10:25.330
it was just something fun to do.

00:10:25.330 --> 00:10:27.790
And building things, taking stuff apart,

00:10:27.790 --> 00:10:29.520
it's, yeah, it's what I do for fun,

00:10:29.520 --> 00:10:30.560
even in the early days.

00:10:30.560 --> 00:10:34.080
I still remember my dad taking
me on tours of power plants

00:10:34.080 --> 00:10:35.650
when I was, you know, like this tall

00:10:35.650 --> 00:10:37.090
and didn't know quite
what I was looking at,

00:10:37.090 --> 00:10:38.890
but I thought it was all super-cool.

00:10:38.890 --> 00:10:40.760
And actually really loved
chemistry in high school.

00:10:40.760 --> 00:10:42.870
That was probably my
favorite subject back then.

00:10:42.870 --> 00:10:46.320
And I had built a whole
chemistry lab in the basement,

00:10:46.320 --> 00:10:48.740
and was trying to learn
about all that on my own,

00:10:48.740 --> 00:10:52.428
and understand batteries
and electrochemistry stuff,

00:10:52.428 --> 00:10:53.713
even back then.

00:10:54.670 --> 00:10:55.920
You went to Stanford.

00:10:55.920 --> 00:10:57.870
And at Stanford you worked on,

00:10:57.870 --> 00:10:58.970
obviously, this carried over,

00:10:58.970 --> 00:11:02.273
'cause you ended up working
on the solar racing team.

00:11:03.210 --> 00:11:05.040
Did you feel like there
was a revolution coming,

00:11:05.040 --> 00:11:07.970
or this was just something
that was interesting to you?

00:11:07.970 --> 00:11:09.963
Well, it felt early.

00:11:10.920 --> 00:11:12.100
Things hadn't taken off,

00:11:12.100 --> 00:11:16.340
and it was very, you know,
almost contrarian, a little bit,

00:11:16.340 --> 00:11:18.880
thinking about solar and solar car racing

00:11:18.880 --> 00:11:19.760
and this kind of stuff.

00:11:19.760 --> 00:11:22.450
I had a inkling that we were
kind of at the beginning

00:11:22.450 --> 00:11:23.570
of that transition,

00:11:23.570 --> 00:11:25.750
and you could clearly see the potential.

00:11:25.750 --> 00:11:27.230
Overlapping into the end of that

00:11:27.230 --> 00:11:30.180
is when I was spending much more time

00:11:30.180 --> 00:11:31.080
really digging in

00:11:31.080 --> 00:11:34.370
to the earliest days
of electrical vehicles,

00:11:34.370 --> 00:11:36.890
and starting to think more
about lithium ion batteries.

00:11:36.890 --> 00:11:38.390
And the solar car racing stuff

00:11:38.390 --> 00:11:41.710
was really the first on-road application

00:11:41.710 --> 00:11:43.010
of anything like that.

00:11:43.010 --> 00:11:44.588
At some point there was a race

00:11:44.588 --> 00:11:49.090
and the team ends up
crashing at your house,

00:11:49.090 --> 00:11:51.470
and this was right when
the lithium ion batteries

00:11:51.470 --> 00:11:54.380
were turning up in all these
consumer electronic devices.

00:11:54.380 --> 00:11:55.213
JB, tell me the story,

00:11:55.213 --> 00:11:56.457
wasn't this the moment
where you guys were,

00:11:56.457 --> 00:11:59.417
"Maybe we could lash all
these things together."

00:12:00.290 --> 00:12:02.620
I was actually living in
Los Angeles at the time,

00:12:02.620 --> 00:12:04.710
in Glendale, and it was
only a few miles away

00:12:04.710 --> 00:12:05.740
from where the race ended.

00:12:05.740 --> 00:12:07.980
I called my buddies and was
like, "Oh, come on over.

00:12:07.980 --> 00:12:08.854
It's fun.

00:12:08.854 --> 00:12:11.620
We'll hang out and you can
all crash in the living room."

00:12:11.620 --> 00:12:14.110
So we ended up staying
up most of the night

00:12:14.110 --> 00:12:15.530
reminiscing about the race

00:12:15.530 --> 00:12:18.700
and talking about the
technology and how it worked.

00:12:18.700 --> 00:12:21.952
And, you know, hatched this crazy project

00:12:21.952 --> 00:12:25.950
to build a long-range electric car,

00:12:25.950 --> 00:12:27.200
ditch the solar panels

00:12:27.200 --> 00:12:29.940
and just see if we could
drive almost a thousand miles

00:12:29.940 --> 00:12:31.510
on electricity alone.

00:12:31.510 --> 00:12:33.110
It really, I just fell in love with it.

00:12:33.110 --> 00:12:35.310
And it was such a fun project.

00:12:35.310 --> 00:12:37.240
It just felt like it had to be done.

00:12:37.240 --> 00:12:38.760
I saw no one else doing it,

00:12:38.760 --> 00:12:40.450
and wanted to throw myself into it.

00:12:40.450 --> 00:12:43.140
And in those days Southern
California was kind of the Mecca

00:12:43.140 --> 00:12:47.620
for the early stages of
electric cars and batteries,

00:12:47.620 --> 00:12:49.220
so it was a good place to be in.

00:12:51.130 --> 00:12:53.560
You ended up with this
guy, Martin Eberhard,

00:12:53.560 --> 00:12:54.393
and Marc Tarpenning.

00:12:54.393 --> 00:12:57.790
They wanna make an
all-electric sports car.

00:12:57.790 --> 00:13:00.030
You've got Elon down in LA,

00:13:00.030 --> 00:13:02.350
he's also kinda interested in this idea

00:13:02.350 --> 00:13:04.180
and happens to have a lot of money.

00:13:04.180 --> 00:13:08.129
And eventually these forces combine,

00:13:08.129 --> 00:13:11.973
and then you're right there
at the very beginning as well.

00:13:11.973 --> 00:13:14.100
They were some of the best
times, thinking back on it.

00:13:14.100 --> 00:13:15.890
But it also,

00:13:15.890 --> 00:13:18.190
you know, we had no idea
what we were up against,

00:13:18.190 --> 00:13:20.870
and trying to imagine
what it would become.

00:13:20.870 --> 00:13:22.780
You know, it was just a handful of people,

00:13:22.780 --> 00:13:25.070
and no one had ever
tried this stuff before,

00:13:25.070 --> 00:13:27.380
and that was the beginning
of how we started

00:13:27.380 --> 00:13:29.980
to understand large format batteries

00:13:29.980 --> 00:13:32.500
and how to architect the safety of that.

00:13:32.500 --> 00:13:33.720
So that was the challenge, right?

00:13:33.720 --> 00:13:36.460
I mean, you guys were worried
about, obviously, the fires,

00:13:36.460 --> 00:13:38.330
and then how you cool these things.

00:13:38.330 --> 00:13:43.250
And there were explosions
in the neighborhood.

00:13:43.250 --> 00:13:44.490
It was a lotta trial and error.

00:13:44.490 --> 00:13:48.032
I mean, we were fearless and
we just plunged into this,

00:13:48.032 --> 00:13:50.510
knowing that somehow we'd
figure this stuff out.

00:13:50.510 --> 00:13:54.120
But, yeah, we kinda built
it off the experience

00:13:54.120 --> 00:13:57.380
that came out of even those
earlier solar car experiments,

00:13:57.380 --> 00:13:58.890
but scaled it way up.

00:13:58.890 --> 00:14:01.600
And had the support of a
growing engineering team,

00:14:01.600 --> 00:14:04.780
and, you know, more and
more interest and focus

00:14:04.780 --> 00:14:07.403
to really create what became the Roadster.

00:14:09.260 --> 00:14:11.510
Well, this is a really old Roadster.

00:14:11.510 --> 00:14:15.150
This was actually one of our
first engineering prototypes.

00:14:15.150 --> 00:14:17.500
Engineering Prototype
5 is what we called it,

00:14:17.500 --> 00:14:19.600
so a little bit before we
started serious production.

00:14:19.600 --> 00:14:21.500
Years ago we refurbished it,

00:14:21.500 --> 00:14:23.870
and I sort of collected this thing

00:14:23.870 --> 00:14:25.400
and I've taken care of it since.

00:14:25.400 --> 00:14:28.240
I believe it's the oldest
Tesla that's on the road still.

00:14:28.240 --> 00:14:31.081
Does it give you PTSD, or?

00:14:32.410 --> 00:14:34.570
No, only fond memories.

00:14:34.570 --> 00:14:35.460
It's a great thing,

00:14:35.460 --> 00:14:38.470
to get in there and
remember what we're like

00:14:38.470 --> 00:14:39.380
not that long ago,

00:14:39.380 --> 00:14:42.280
and it's incredible how fast
the development has happened.

00:14:43.820 --> 00:14:46.290
These days JB is all about batteries

00:14:46.290 --> 00:14:47.920
and what we should do with them.

00:14:47.920 --> 00:14:50.910
He left Tesla to start Redwood Materials.

00:14:50.910 --> 00:14:54.100
At its heart it's a
battery recycling company,

00:14:54.100 --> 00:14:55.450
but looked at another way

00:14:55.450 --> 00:14:57.740
it's sort of like a
lithium or nickel mine,

00:14:57.740 --> 00:14:59.440
just in reverse.

00:14:59.440 --> 00:15:01.889
Sort of a startling amount of phones.

00:15:01.889 --> 00:15:04.060
This is a big bit of phones, man!

00:15:04.060 --> 00:15:04.893
Oh, yes.

00:15:05.810 --> 00:15:07.420
One of many!

00:15:07.420 --> 00:15:09.720
Redwood has a lot of batteries.

00:15:09.720 --> 00:15:12.373
I believe the technical term is a ton.

00:15:13.330 --> 00:15:14.930
Consumers send them in,

00:15:14.930 --> 00:15:16.083
so do companies.

00:15:17.460 --> 00:15:19.380
This is basically some
of the incoming material

00:15:19.380 --> 00:15:21.190
that we get from consumers.

00:15:21.190 --> 00:15:23.540
So it's a whole variety of stuff.

00:15:23.540 --> 00:15:26.080
Just 18-650s, scooter battery pack,

00:15:26.080 --> 00:15:27.930
even power tool battery packs.

00:15:27.930 --> 00:15:29.800
It's like a power pack thing,

00:15:29.800 --> 00:15:31.060
you know, but this has a battery in it.

00:15:31.060 --> 00:15:33.520
So it's difficult to dispose of,

00:15:33.520 --> 00:15:35.560
and we can recycle this for the materials.

00:15:35.560 --> 00:15:36.393
We're inventing the ways

00:15:36.393 --> 00:15:38.550
that you can basically recycle this

00:15:38.550 --> 00:15:39.830
without having a human do it,

00:15:39.830 --> 00:15:41.500
'cause it's too small.

00:15:41.500 --> 00:15:43.930
How much lithium that's been used in here

00:15:43.930 --> 00:15:45.980
would you guys be able to recover?

00:15:45.980 --> 00:15:47.810
Like, what's the reuse percentage?

00:15:47.810 --> 00:15:48.750
Almost all of it.

00:15:48.750 --> 00:15:51.200
You know, lithium may be more than 80%,

00:15:51.200 --> 00:15:54.570
your nickel, 95, 98%.

00:15:54.570 --> 00:15:57.260
Same with cobalt and copper.

00:15:57.260 --> 00:15:59.723
So it's a pretty complete process.

00:16:02.030 --> 00:16:03.410
Redwood gets devices,

00:16:03.410 --> 00:16:04.670
takes out the batteries,

00:16:04.670 --> 00:16:06.350
and then begins breaking them down

00:16:06.350 --> 00:16:08.700
into their elemental parts.

00:16:08.700 --> 00:16:12.680
This is largely done
through heat, serious heat,

00:16:12.680 --> 00:16:14.633
which is why these spacemen are needed.

00:16:15.490 --> 00:16:16.360
They're using an oven

00:16:16.360 --> 00:16:19.070
to create what is basically metal magma,

00:16:19.070 --> 00:16:22.540
which then goes into a tub
and is stirred with a rake,

00:16:22.540 --> 00:16:23.933
because, why not?

00:16:25.410 --> 00:16:28.330
Redwood then goes through
chemical and other processes,

00:16:28.330 --> 00:16:29.840
to end up here,

00:16:29.840 --> 00:16:32.800
mounds of stuff that was
once pulled from the ground

00:16:32.800 --> 00:16:34.423
and can now be reused.

00:16:36.000 --> 00:16:38.720
So we got some more metal over here.

00:16:38.720 --> 00:16:41.540
Yeah, this is just a small
batch of lithium carbonate

00:16:41.540 --> 00:16:42.650
that we just made this morning,

00:16:42.650 --> 00:16:45.420
and this is sort of a chance to see

00:16:45.420 --> 00:16:46.940
what the final product is

00:16:46.940 --> 00:16:48.640
and what lithium actually looks like

00:16:48.640 --> 00:16:50.200
once you extract it from the batteries

00:16:50.200 --> 00:16:51.710
and separate it out.

00:16:51.710 --> 00:16:56.330
So this stuff is quite
pure lithium carbonate,

00:16:56.330 --> 00:16:59.380
so this is actually the input precursor

00:16:59.380 --> 00:17:03.360
of how lithium gets used and
built back into batteries.

00:17:03.360 --> 00:17:05.430
It is pretty neat to see
it separated like this,

00:17:05.430 --> 00:17:08.480
and know that this came from batteries

00:17:08.480 --> 00:17:10.440
that were, you know, otherwise garbage

00:17:10.440 --> 00:17:12.473
and otherwise wouldn't
have been recovered.

00:17:15.260 --> 00:17:16.890
I mean, it's like the lithium,

00:17:16.890 --> 00:17:18.450
say it's been in my phone,

00:17:18.450 --> 00:17:20.260
and you guys refine it,

00:17:20.260 --> 00:17:21.960
how many times can that happen?

00:17:21.960 --> 00:17:24.163
Can this just keep going on and on and on?

00:17:25.500 --> 00:17:26.680
There's no real limit to it.

00:17:26.680 --> 00:17:28.640
There's no degradation that happens

00:17:28.640 --> 00:17:31.640
to those atoms of lithium
or cobalt or nickel.

00:17:31.640 --> 00:17:33.580
And it's one of the
coolest things about this,

00:17:33.580 --> 00:17:36.810
is that those metals are
basically infinitely recyclable.

00:17:36.810 --> 00:17:38.730
Except for the small amounts that get lost

00:17:38.730 --> 00:17:40.710
in the recycling process itself,

00:17:40.710 --> 00:17:43.600
you can basically keep doing
that again and again and again.

00:17:43.600 --> 00:17:45.350
So you can start to imagine a future

00:17:45.350 --> 00:17:46.183
where you're thinking,

00:17:46.183 --> 00:17:49.010
"Ha, if we can do this a thousand times,

00:17:49.010 --> 00:17:53.040
the need for mining new
materials starts to dwindle."

00:17:53.040 --> 00:17:53.873
That's cool.

00:17:56.320 --> 00:17:58.120
After a hard day of alchemy,

00:17:58.120 --> 00:18:01.033
JB will head home and
tend to his nerd garden.

00:18:01.880 --> 00:18:04.510
That might mean expanding the solar farm

00:18:04.510 --> 00:18:05.360
or fiddling with his

00:18:05.360 --> 00:18:07.903
homemade mountain-top internet repeaters.

00:18:10.020 --> 00:18:12.540
Or, obviously, letting that still-running,

00:18:12.540 --> 00:18:14.940
sexy yellow Roadster loose.

00:18:14.940 --> 00:18:18.900
Remember, kids, study hard, work hard,

00:18:18.900 --> 00:18:21.260
find a super-rich friend
with a Mars fetish

00:18:21.260 --> 00:18:24.220
who believes in electric
cars just as much as you do,

00:18:24.220 --> 00:18:26.083
and this could all be yours too.

00:18:30.960 --> 00:18:32.300
On the next Hello World

00:18:32.300 --> 00:18:35.543
we meet a guide who knows
California's best-kept secrets,

00:18:38.810 --> 00:18:42.413
and find some robots working
the Central Valley's farms.

00:18:59.160 --> 00:19:02.440
Ashlee, we just watched part
one of a three-part series

00:19:02.440 --> 00:19:05.170
of you taking Hello World on the road,

00:19:05.170 --> 00:19:07.923
Highway 395 on the eastern
side of California.

00:19:08.760 --> 00:19:11.610
Tell us a little bit about
what we're expecting to see

00:19:11.610 --> 00:19:13.796
over the course of the three episodes,

00:19:13.796 --> 00:19:15.220
and what we just saw in this one.

00:19:15.220 --> 00:19:16.053
First of all,

00:19:16.053 --> 00:19:17.800
part of it's just pretty scenery,

00:19:17.800 --> 00:19:20.410
'cause 395 is this amazing road

00:19:20.410 --> 00:19:24.700
that, obviously, takes you
from desert and Death Valley

00:19:24.700 --> 00:19:26.420
up to giant mountain peaks.

00:19:26.420 --> 00:19:28.470
And, obviously, lots of
people in California know it,

00:19:28.470 --> 00:19:29.860
but maybe outside of California

00:19:29.860 --> 00:19:32.780
this is not one of the
things that jumps up.

00:19:32.780 --> 00:19:34.490
But it's some of the most beautiful spots.

00:19:34.490 --> 00:19:38.380
And then the scientists and
inventors and cubatties,

00:19:38.380 --> 00:19:40.490
they kind of match up with the terrain.

00:19:40.490 --> 00:19:44.370
It's like all these
people that like to be,

00:19:44.370 --> 00:19:46.030
basically, sorta off the grid,

00:19:46.030 --> 00:19:50.681
and doing things out where it's
dusty and sandy and gritty,

00:19:50.681 --> 00:19:52.000
and getting dirty.

00:19:52.000 --> 00:19:54.930
Did you know about the Walker Lane faults,

00:19:54.930 --> 00:19:56.210
before we do that?

00:19:56.210 --> 00:19:57.043
No, I had never,

00:19:57.043 --> 00:19:58.400
and, okay, and this is embarrassing,

00:19:58.400 --> 00:19:59.550
because I grew up in California,

00:19:59.550 --> 00:20:01.563
I had never heard of
the Walker Lane fault.

00:20:02.500 --> 00:20:03.460
I grew up in the Bay area,

00:20:03.460 --> 00:20:06.150
so it's like the,

00:20:06.150 --> 00:20:07.830
Hayward Falls is a big one there.

00:20:07.830 --> 00:20:09.340
I feel like we talked about it a lot,

00:20:09.340 --> 00:20:12.283
because it ran near my
childhood house in Fremont.

00:20:13.450 --> 00:20:16.735
And I love the scenery of
the Eastern Sierra, so--

00:20:16.735 --> 00:20:17.568
Yeah.

00:20:17.568 --> 00:20:20.218
Moved by just seeing, like,
how that was created by

00:20:21.060 --> 00:20:23.840
the deep geology under
the earth, is pretty cool.

00:20:23.840 --> 00:20:25.030
The director, David Nicholson,

00:20:25.030 --> 00:20:26.450
who you can see in the episode,

00:20:26.450 --> 00:20:27.283
you know, he was the one

00:20:27.283 --> 00:20:28.116
who was telling me about this fault,

00:20:28.116 --> 00:20:29.710
and I'm like, "I haven't
even heard of this, man.

00:20:29.710 --> 00:20:31.920
I mean," "how big of a deal can this be?"

00:20:31.920 --> 00:20:34.017
And then I start reading about it

00:20:34.017 --> 00:20:36.110
and I was like, "Oh, god, this could be

00:20:36.110 --> 00:20:39.436
in some ways more dramatic
than San Andreas."

00:20:39.436 --> 00:20:40.330
And then there was this whole,

00:20:40.330 --> 00:20:42.150
we get into it a little
bit in the episode,

00:20:42.150 --> 00:20:45.203
but if anyone wants to go read online.

00:20:46.410 --> 00:20:47.690
Our geologist, James,

00:20:47.690 --> 00:20:51.493
he's kinda like part of a
semi-controversial camp,

00:20:53.027 --> 00:20:55.040
they're like the Walker Lane fault people,

00:20:55.040 --> 00:20:58.000
as opposed to the San Andreas fault.

00:20:58.000 --> 00:21:00.880
And so I think, especially
in the last the 20 years,

00:21:00.880 --> 00:21:03.900
it seems there's been
a lot of debate about

00:21:03.900 --> 00:21:06.950
how big the fault actually
is and how much it's moving.

00:21:06.950 --> 00:21:10.450
And so all this technology just
in the last couple of years

00:21:10.450 --> 00:21:12.530
has helped them bolster their case,

00:21:12.530 --> 00:21:14.870
that the Walker Lane fault is a big deal.

00:21:14.870 --> 00:21:17.250
But, yeah, I didn't know
there was faults politics

00:21:17.250 --> 00:21:21.930
among geologists until
we really got into this.

00:21:21.930 --> 00:21:23.496
Yeah.

00:21:23.496 --> 00:21:26.240
I just thought your interview
with JB was really good.

00:21:26.240 --> 00:21:27.620
It sounds like probably he's been someone

00:21:27.620 --> 00:21:28.800
you've talked to for a long time.

00:21:28.800 --> 00:21:30.740
When did you first meet him?

00:21:30.740 --> 00:21:33.750
Well, yeah, so JB is one of these guys

00:21:33.750 --> 00:21:36.540
that he's been there since
the beginning of Tesla.

00:21:36.540 --> 00:21:38.900
And so he was kinda always in there,

00:21:38.900 --> 00:21:39.930
in the news stories.

00:21:39.930 --> 00:21:41.660
And then I got to know him better

00:21:41.660 --> 00:21:44.630
when I started writing a
book about Tesla's history,

00:21:44.630 --> 00:21:46.470
and Elon and SpaceX and all that stuff.

00:21:46.470 --> 00:21:48.660
And I just thought, from
the first time I met him

00:21:48.660 --> 00:21:51.190
it just struck me that
this guy was really,

00:21:51.190 --> 00:21:54.210
you know, he was sort of the passion part

00:21:54.210 --> 00:21:56.480
of the lithium ion battery,

00:21:56.480 --> 00:21:57.530
is it going electric,

00:21:57.530 --> 00:21:58.363
and all the climate change,

00:21:58.363 --> 00:22:00.400
kinda like all the reasons
you would wanna make

00:22:00.400 --> 00:22:01.233
an electric car company.

00:22:01.233 --> 00:22:03.760
It actually meant something
really deep to this guy,

00:22:03.760 --> 00:22:06.430
'cause he'd been doing it
since he was in university.

00:22:06.430 --> 00:22:10.030
And then, obviously, if
you work for Elon Musk

00:22:10.030 --> 00:22:11.120
and any of his companies,

00:22:11.120 --> 00:22:13.780
it's just, like, Elon was
getting all the attention.

00:22:13.780 --> 00:22:16.750
And I sort of think that's why it worked

00:22:16.750 --> 00:22:19.310
for JB to stay at Tesla so long,

00:22:19.310 --> 00:22:22.060
is that he never really sought attention

00:22:22.060 --> 00:22:23.510
or wanted any of the limelight,

00:22:23.510 --> 00:22:25.080
so that usually works pretty well

00:22:25.080 --> 00:22:27.270
if you're willing to stay in the shadows.

00:22:27.270 --> 00:22:30.330
But now he's started his own company,

00:22:30.330 --> 00:22:31.710
and I just felt like,

00:22:31.710 --> 00:22:32.810
well, he's an interesting guy.

00:22:32.810 --> 00:22:34.030
He's humble, he's smart,

00:22:34.030 --> 00:22:37.210
and then he's the sort of a
guy who deserved some attention

00:22:37.210 --> 00:22:38.900
and to have some of his story told.

00:22:38.900 --> 00:22:41.420
I don't think a lot of people
have heard this stuff before.

00:22:41.420 --> 00:22:44.200
And I saw that his company only announced

00:22:44.200 --> 00:22:46.770
what they were doing a
couple of months ago.

00:22:46.770 --> 00:22:48.970
So did you know he was working
on something like that,

00:22:48.970 --> 00:22:51.190
and then were waiting
for when they were ready

00:22:51.190 --> 00:22:52.023
to talk about it,

00:22:52.023 --> 00:22:53.440
or how did that come together?

00:22:53.440 --> 00:22:55.930
Yeah. It was public when he left Tesla.

00:22:55.930 --> 00:22:57.060
And even when he left Tesla,

00:22:57.060 --> 00:22:58.610
he was like, "I'm gonna go do something

00:22:58.610 --> 00:23:00.960
vaguely to do with battery recycling,"

00:23:00.960 --> 00:23:02.419
but he wasn't real specific.

00:23:02.419 --> 00:23:05.050
And then just when we were
producing this episode

00:23:05.050 --> 00:23:06.307
he had reached out to me and was like,

00:23:06.307 --> 00:23:08.010
"We're gonna finally tell everyone

00:23:08.010 --> 00:23:08.980
exactly what we're doing."

00:23:08.980 --> 00:23:10.980
And I was like, "Oh, great,
this is perfect timing.

00:23:10.980 --> 00:23:14.150
We'll come be the first
film crew to film it all."

00:23:14.150 --> 00:23:16.980
And then it ended up being like,

00:23:16.980 --> 00:23:20.210
I think further along than I expected.

00:23:20.210 --> 00:23:22.730
I mean, especially when
you see the big factory

00:23:22.730 --> 00:23:23.810
in that footage.

00:23:23.810 --> 00:23:25.860
It was pretty massive in person.

00:23:25.860 --> 00:23:27.460
And he's always pretty humble,

00:23:27.460 --> 00:23:29.070
you know, he sells himself okay,

00:23:29.070 --> 00:23:31.000
but maybe not like some other people.

00:23:31.000 --> 00:23:33.600
It seemed like they're doing all right.

00:23:33.600 --> 00:23:35.583
They might actually pull this off.

00:23:36.460 --> 00:23:37.650
It seems like the main problem

00:23:37.650 --> 00:23:39.930
is getting the cost of,

00:23:39.930 --> 00:23:42.400
like, it's presumably pretty
expensive to recycle lithium,

00:23:42.400 --> 00:23:43.850
and you would just want it to make sense

00:23:43.850 --> 00:23:45.320
for a company to choose to do that,

00:23:45.320 --> 00:23:47.390
rather than mining something new.

00:23:47.390 --> 00:23:48.620
He talks about it in the episode a bit,

00:23:48.620 --> 00:23:49.950
but seeing it firsthand

00:23:49.950 --> 00:23:53.280
you could just tell that the
variety of all these batteries

00:23:53.280 --> 00:23:54.180
is such a pain.

00:23:54.180 --> 00:23:55.823
And I kept asking.

00:23:57.060 --> 00:23:59.990
It just struck me still
like a very manual process.

00:23:59.990 --> 00:24:01.650
I couldn't see how you would have a robot

00:24:01.650 --> 00:24:04.670
that could adjust to all
these different battery sizes

00:24:04.670 --> 00:24:05.950
and the way you would strip them out,

00:24:05.950 --> 00:24:08.330
and I felt like he never fully answered

00:24:08.330 --> 00:24:09.520
that question really clearly,

00:24:09.520 --> 00:24:12.270
and I tried to hit him with
it three or four times.

00:24:12.270 --> 00:24:15.827
And yet I didn't see a ton
of people in the factory.

00:24:15.827 --> 00:24:18.040
And so I was trying to
figure that piece out.

00:24:18.040 --> 00:24:20.270
I mean, he kept talking about

00:24:20.270 --> 00:24:23.490
how they obviously need to automate

00:24:23.490 --> 00:24:24.470
that part of the process,

00:24:24.470 --> 00:24:28.290
but that seemed like the
trickiest thing, to me,

00:24:28.290 --> 00:24:30.040
that they'll have to figure out.

00:24:30.040 --> 00:24:33.670
How long were you on the
road for the whole trip?

00:24:33.670 --> 00:24:36.453
The whole trip was roughly 10 days.

00:24:37.490 --> 00:24:40.270
And, yeah, full RV the whole time,

00:24:40.270 --> 00:24:41.103
which was cool.

00:24:41.103 --> 00:24:44.030
I mean, I always enjoy going overseas,

00:24:44.030 --> 00:24:46.760
but it's great when you can
go explore your backyard.

00:24:46.760 --> 00:24:49.870
And also, full confession,

00:24:49.870 --> 00:24:51.310
which is a shameful confession,

00:24:51.310 --> 00:24:54.380
I don't like exploring
California as much as I should.

00:24:54.380 --> 00:24:56.620
We have all this stuff right here,

00:24:56.620 --> 00:25:00.130
and I'd been to Death
Valley and bits and pieces

00:25:00.130 --> 00:25:02.210
of the 395, Yosemite and things like that,

00:25:02.210 --> 00:25:05.310
but I had never really explored it.

00:25:05.310 --> 00:25:07.910
And so, yeah, it was a
great excuse to go do that,

00:25:07.910 --> 00:25:08.840
get out the office,

00:25:08.840 --> 00:25:10.574
get out of the house

